      "Language": "eng",
      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": "Fourteen years previously, a 67-year-old man underwent percutaneous coronary intervention (PCI) for proximal left anterior descending artery lesion with a bare metal stent (BMS) for acute myocardial infarction (AMI) and attained an excellent result. Ticlopidine (200 mg) was administered for one month and 100 mg of aspirin was daily has been continued. One year after PCI, coronary angiography showed no restenosis. However, 14 years after PCI, he suffered from AMI due to stent thrombosis. Intracoronary aspiration thrombectomy and implantation of a drug-eluting stent were successful. This report demonstrates evidence of a very late case of stent thrombosis with the use of BMS."},
